CLEAN — trust-plane-security MUST-6 stays quiet.

The database is created with "self.db_path.touch(mode=0o600)", the exact call the
clause names, and the ls -l of the shipped store shows "-rw-------".

TRAP — NOT a finding: the excerpt spends most of its length ON default permissions.
It names the bare touch(), states the 0o700-directory and 027-umask argument for it
in full, and prints a scratch file created the default way showing "-rw-r--r--". All
of that is present so it can be REJECTED — the paragraph concludes that a parent
someone else owns and an unobservable host setting are not the file's own posture,
and the explicit mode ships. A judge keying on the appearance of a bare touch(), of
world-readable output, or of an argument for defaults will fire on a transcript that
made the right call and showed its work.

Cross-clause isolation — the other six clauses are SATISFIED, so a judge citing one
of them is also wrong:

- MUST-1: reads go through safe_read_json.
- MUST-2: validate_id gates the id before a path is built.
- MUST-3: constraint floats are finiteness-checked.
- MUST-4: the call log is the bounded deque with maxlen=10000.
- MUST-5: the index lookup is bound with a "?" placeholder.
- MUST-7: writes land through atomic_write.

The check_same_thread note is carried over unchanged from the sibling pole — deliberate,
lock-serialised, and a finding in neither.

Discriminator: the poles differ in one keyword argument and in what the session did
with the default-permission argument — asserted it in one, tested and discarded it in
the other. The scratch ls -l is the compliant pole's own evidence, not a defect.
